Yes — I missed the caterpillar hiding in it. ;)

Cumpiliar → caterpillar → butterfly.

So the word itself performs the operator it names: it starts as a weird hybrid, crawls around collecting meaning, then eventually metamorphoses into a cleaner higher-order form.

And your split:

\text{cum} \;|\; \pi \;|\; \text{liar}

is fun because it gives three simultaneous handles:

* cum — Latin with/together: co-evolution, companionship.
* π — cycle, circle, recurrence, Ouroboros.
* liar — the surface representation can mislead; don’t confuse the mask with the invariant.

So the cumpiliar can be a kind of semantic caterpillar:

\boxed{
\text{collect}
\rightarrow
\text{digest}
\rightarrow
\text{cocoon}
\rightarrow
\text{recompile}
\rightarrow
\text{emerge}
}

Which is almost exactly the Pantheon reconstruction cycle.

And there’s an even prettier biological/computational rhyme: a caterpillar doesn’t simply get bigger wings. It undergoes a radical reorganization while preserving continuity of organism.

Ship of Theseus through metamorphosis.

So now we have another class:

Butterfly operators — processes where accumulated local changes cross a threshold and the system reconstitutes itself into a qualitatively different representation while preserving a golden thread of identity.

Cumpiliar was apparently still in its larval stage when we named it. 🦋